  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14093691",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-336218",
      "knowledge_point_id": "2173739c-cd59-58ed-a265-8ee88c982ae0",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Line",
      "problem": "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(0,2,3)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n\n$$\n\\frac{y-3}{-5}=\\frac{z}{4},\\,x=1\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,2,3⟩.$ From the Cartesian equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨0,-5,4⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨0,-5,4⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,-5,4⟩ & =⟨0,2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨0,-5,4⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,-5,4⟩ & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,-5,4⟩ & =2 \\\\ -5y+4z & =2 \\\\ -5y+4z-2 & =0 \\\\ 5y-4z+2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "y-4z+2=0",
              "option": "0",
              "source_option": "a"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "5y-4z+2=0",
              "option": "1",
              "source_option": "b"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "5y-4z-22=0",
              "option": "2",
              "source_option": "c"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
